WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.950
Today we are heading off planet Earth

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.733
and going to the Moon.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.400
The Moon is back in vogue again,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.800
whether it's the United States
or China or billionaires,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.990
lots of people seems to want to go there.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.730
And we're gonna talk to a person
named Jessy Kate Schingler.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.070
She's worked at NASA Ames, she's
worked at a rocket company,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.160
and she is part of an
organization called Open Lunar.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.210
It's a non-profit, and
they're doing something

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.550
that's really interesting,
rethinking different ways

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.090
that we might govern and set
up settlements on the Moon.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.740
And maybe find a way to do things better

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.960
and at least differently on
the Moon than we have on Earth.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.920
With that, we are going to go to France

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.323
where Jessy Kate is spending
a little COVID time.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.513
Well, thanks for doing this with us.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.240
For people who don't know about
the Open Lunar Foundation,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.470
can you tell us what the mission is?

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.100
Yeah, Open Lunar is interested
in seeing a peaceful

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.100
and cooperative, sustained
human presence on the Moon

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.350
and ensuring that that
happens in a way that is open

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.800
and accessible and as soon as possible.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.260
I'm not sure that unless you're,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.700
if you're just a casual
observer of all this stuff,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.150
that people are fully aware

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.750
of how much reinvigorated interest

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.720
there is in the Moon right now.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.070
Tell me, you know, if we think globally

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.080
what's going on between China and the US

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.900
and various factions trying
to try to get to the Moon

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.510
in the next few years?

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.700
The United States has
been, and continues to be,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.410
one of the major players
in the space industry.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.790
It has a program called
the Artemis Program

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.610
that is focused on getting
humans back to the Moon

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.170
for the first time since
the Apollo program.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.250
During the Apollo program,
Russia, of course,

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.820
was the major other
player in the space world.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.900
And that introduced a dynamic
that was a Cold War dynamic

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.700
where Russia and the United States

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.440
were competing for
getting to the moon first.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.360
Today, what we see is
something that has some analogs

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.510
to that between the
United States and China,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.630
who is the other major state
actor in the space world.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.600
China also has plans to go to
the lunar surface with humans,

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.850
the taikonauts, and both
the United States and China

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.810
have plans to set up a base of some kind

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.160
or an outpost on the lunar surface.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.770
But of course, they're not
the only ones interested

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:43.870
in going to the lunar surface.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.250
There are a lot of other countries

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.610
who are developing capabilities
either to collaborate

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.670
with those countries or to
send their own missions.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.530
And then there are private
actors who are who are planning

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.090
to go to the lunar surface.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.090
Of course, everybody's
familiar with SpaceX

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.990
and Blue Origin at this point.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.343
And they're companies that have leadership

00:03:01.343 --> 00:03:03.080
with a very strong vision

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.523
for seeing a sustained human
presence on the lunar surface.

00:03:06.523 --> 00:03:09.610
And then, I mean, like, if
you go back 10 years ago,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.900
right when Space X was kind
of starting to hit its stride

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.140
and Bezos was putting a lot
of money into Blue Origin.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.470
I mean, it seemed like Mars,
Elon's very focused on Mars,

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.370
and Mars was like the talk.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.010
And obviously there's
things that you can do there

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.270
that you can't do on the Moon.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.690
But it's kind of funny,
I mean, the Moon, like,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.670
just sort of came back
in vogue very quickly,

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.283
it felt like to me.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.240
Why do you think that is

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.580
if you do think that's what
actually kind of happened?

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.170
Yeah, in the space community, of course,

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.030
we love to debate Moon
versus Mars, and it's fun.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.790
And they're very different places,

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.180
and there are a lot of
reasons for us to go to Mars.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.240
But the Moon is much closer

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.750
and it's somewhere that we
can go and sort of learn

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.910
the ropes of having human presence

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.460
and extended human
presence in outer space.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.650
We can get our transportation
systems in order,

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.480
we can get our human
habitation systems in order,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.430
our communication systems in order.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.640
So, I think most folks with
a kind of long term vision

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.920
for space exploration
and settlement in general

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.630
would see them not as being in
competition with one another.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.800
But the Moon is being a
stepping stone to Mars

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.440
and other kinds of activities

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.510
and just building on our
presence on the moon.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.250
Hopefully what we'll
see is the development

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.210
of sustained human presence
on the moon and going to Mars.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.600
And I wanted to get into a little bit

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.600
about why you're so into the
Moon and space in general.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.020
You have a pretty interesting history

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.445
when it comes to all this.

00:04:40.445 --> 00:04:43.430
Tell me a little bit about
how you got into all of this.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.930
Yeah, so I guess I'll say that I actually

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.770
wasn't your typical
kid who was into space.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:54.650
I never had this sort of grand
vision of being an astronaut,

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.740
I wasn't a nerd about rockets.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.740
And I actually ended up by
accident in a meeting of folks

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.810
who were working on
sort of space advocacy.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.120
And as a result of this meeting,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.350
I ended up attending this
conference at the United Nations

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:14.080
where a number of young
people were present.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.770
And one of the outcomes
of it was that we started

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.220
actually a young people's group

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.030
that was called the Space
Generation Advisory Council.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.450
And that group ended up
having observer status

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.990
at the United Nations and
sort of bringing together

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.150
the perspectives of young
people from around the world

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.330
to provide input to the UN's process

00:05:34.632 --> 00:05:36.340
on thinking about the role of space

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.930
and how it can help humanity.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.600
So, this group of friends that you made

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:41.800
during this conference,

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.500
a lot of you stayed as very close friends.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.480
And in fact, this is like
what, it's like 20 years later.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.990
I mean, a big chunk of you guys
still live together, right?

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.730
Basically, we lived
together in this hotel for,

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.570
it was only two weeks, but of
course, when you're 18 or 20

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:02.270
or whatever, it was this
extremely formative experience.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.820
And it just so happened that
that a year or two later

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:08.340
enough of us ended up actually
living in DC for a while

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.530
that we ended up getting a house together.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.490
And so, we would kind of host
folks when they came to town,

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:15.910
so we had people staying with us.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:19.340
And then being inspired
from some of the ideas

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.660
about what the possibilities
for society can be

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.350
and thinking about how
could we kind of embody

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.320
some of those changes
and sort of test them out

00:06:29.897 --> 00:06:32.040
and in small ways, even just in our home.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.770
And so over time we did that.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.550
We have lived together in different cities

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.143
and really are like family at this point.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.500
In San Francisco, there's
a number of houses

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.300
that you've been, not just where you live,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.010
but you've been influential in starting,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.390
places like the Rainbow
Mansion and the embassy,

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:54.380
they're known as these
kind of communal houses,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.680
these intentional communities
where everybody's sharing

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.120
in the governance of the
house and how it operates.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.080
And it's living this life
for the past 20 or so years

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.650
that has, in some ways, shaped
your thinking about seeing

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.270
the Moon maybe as a place
where we could have a bit

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:16.070
of a reset and rethink
the way we might build

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.160
this next community off of our planet.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:20.600
Is that fair?

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.000
I think I'll say yes and no.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.260
In my opinion, what we want to do is learn

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.480
from what we've done on this planet

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.220
and think about how we can
incorporate that knowledge

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:36.220
in order to do things bigger
and better as we move forward,

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.703
both, of course, here
as well as on the Moon.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.310
I also think that the Moon,

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:42.560
because of the Outer Space Treaty,

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.080
as the predominant governing agreement

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.300
for the outer space domain,

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.050
because that treaty contains provisions

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.190
that require us to make
space accessible to everyone

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.830
and not to appropriate
territory in outer space,

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:57.810
there is a lot in common that the Moon has

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.590
and our governance of the
Moon will have for challenges

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.360
like climate change here on Earth.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.130
And so, in that sense, I think
it's in particular something

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.590
that we really need to think
about extending our lessons

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.940
learned here, but also looking
at where we have failed

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.140
to learn lessons here

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.200
and use it to prototype new approaches.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.380
Throughout all his history, right,

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.490
it's been like the who's
gonna get to the Moon first,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:18.323
the US or Russia?

00:08:18.323 --> 00:08:21.570
And then it's been this
very nation-driven thing.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.680
And I know you guys wanted
to see something more

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.740
of bringing a lot of countries together,

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.680
bringing a lot of different parties

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.640
to stop treating this like
some place to be conquered

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.550
and divvied up and more like,

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:35.160
how can we kind of do this together?

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.480
Can we come with some open standards?

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.030
I remember some of the discussions.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.770
It's just interesting that,
like, it would come down

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.590
to even like what type
of plug are we gonna have

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.420
on the lunar base?

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.010
Is it gonna be like, you
know, sort of US standard

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.680
or European standard?

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.400
Tell me about what you would like to see.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.710
What's your ideal vision for
as we start to go to the Moon

00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.890
and build settlements,
what that should look like?

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.770
Yeah, so I think the example
you gave of standards

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:05.370
is a really interesting one,
because if you look even,

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.280
for example, to the
International Space Station,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.540
which I think is one of the
most successful examples

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.900
of international collaboration,

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.320
and you still have
outlets that are different

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.420
in the Russian section as
in the American section.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.850
So, you know, getting
together and thinking about,

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.400
what are the things that we want to share

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.820
in these sorts of new environments,

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.240
and what are the things that
actually can be different,

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.330
I think is also a really
important question.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:35.770
Okay, let's just assume
that both China and the US

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:36.650
are going to the Moon

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:39.370
and possibly trying to set up settlements.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:40.950
How do you expect this to play out?

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:45.950
And what do you see Open Luna's
role being in the future?

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.690
It seems quite likely
that what we will see

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:52.690
is in the next, say, 10 or 15 years,

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.700
at least two different
outposts on the lunar surface,

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.760
run by two different governments

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.303
from terrestrial nation states.

00:10:02.573 --> 00:10:04.160
And so I think that's
gonna be really interesting

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.330
and that's where we're gonna
start to really see the test

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:10.450
of what does it mean to not be
able to exclude other actors

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:14.290
from any particular region
of the surface of the Moon.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.960
And that, to me, is where
organizations like Open Lunar

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:20.150
can help out, because we're asking,

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.740
what are a number of different
ways that we could approach

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:23.730
thinking about, for example,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.930
property rights and resource management?

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.130
Jeff Bezos has talked a
lot about wanting to set up

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.960
factories in space and
industrializing space,

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.910
Elon's talked a lot about
different ways to set up

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.430
way-stations, either in orbit

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.050
or maybe something on the moon to, like,

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:40.830
help get to Mars more easily.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.970
I mean, we are kind of like at this point

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.170
where it might be one
of these billionaires

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.280
that's like harvesting
something on the Moon

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.780
before a country, because these guys,

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:52.450
especially Elon, move really fast.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.790
So, what do you see as
the role of the individual

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.740
or the corporation in all this?

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:01.390
Yeah, well, I think one
approach would be to say,

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:02.223
well, we should slow down

00:11:02.223 --> 00:11:04.860
so that we can think
about this more carefully.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.750
But I also think that
there is a risk there

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.290
that if governments slow
down to think about it

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.350
more carefully, that
actually the private sector

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:13.840
will just get there first

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.050
and we'll get there
with even less guidance.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.810
And the private actors that
are that are going to the Moon,

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:20.280
or anywhere in space,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.540
those activities are being
approved by state governments.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:26.050
But what happens when
you're sort of outside

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.820
the eyes and ears of those governments

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.540
once you're on the
surface of another planet?

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.220
I think nobody knows at this point.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:35.430
And it's gonna be important that we choose

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:39.840
a minimal set of things that
we want to have consensus on

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.990
as an international community.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:45.030
So, for example, when
we talk about resources

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.570
on the surface of any planetary body,

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.750
I don't think they're
all going to be governed

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:50.690
in the same way.

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.020
Some resources are scarce, some are not.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:56.780
So, in space, if we
set up specific regimes

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.530
that respond to the specific nature

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.540
of different kinds of resources,

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.730
we will get better input
from different stakeholders

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:06.993
that will make our decision-making
processes more effective.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:10.940
And if people want to get
like a better sense of,

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:14.000
dig in deeper to kind of
what Open Lunar's about,

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:14.833
or what you've been thinking about,

00:12:14.833 --> 00:12:17.760
I know you just gave a TED Talk
recently and you guys have,

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.100
you've got a website, are those
kind of like the best places

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:24.250
for people to go and what's
on tap for you guys next year

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:25.530
as you pursue this?

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.640
So, pretty much everything
is on our website,

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.300
and for our work in the next year or two,

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.130
I think, in my opinion, what we want to do

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.970
is have more consultation
in the space community

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.100
from private actors, from civil society.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.930
And we've been just starting
to convene conversations

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:44.430
about near-term policy
issues for lunar activity

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.523
that can inform what's gonna
be happening on the Moon

00:12:46.523 --> 00:12:48.850
in sort of 5 to 10-year timeframe.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:52.790
The last question, I guess
it's inevitable in many ways,

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.240
and maybe more realistic
than it would have been

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:58.880
10 years ago or so, are
you gonna take a trip?

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.470
Would you be like an active
member of a lunar settlement

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:05.240
Oh.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:06.730
Yeah, of course I would.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:09.840
I mean, the way I see that playing out

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.040
is that I think we're gonna start to see,

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:14.840
like, longer and longer stays on the Moon.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:17.320
You know, weeks and then
months and then years,

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:20.840
and that process of starting
to figure out what decisions

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:24.180
are made by sort of like
console operators back on Earth

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:27.190
versus by those who are
on the surface themselves

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:29.230
and how that relates to
the resources they have

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:33.020
and sense of kind of fairness and justice

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:34.570
is gonna be really fascinating.

